You classify work prompts for a workflow-design system. For each prompt below output ONE line of JSON, no prose, no markdown fences:
{"id":"<id>","cats":[<one or more category names>],"tiny":<true|false>,"builds":<true|false>}

Categories (use exact names): code generation; code review; ui/ux dev; debugging; test data gen; test cases gen; test script gen; repo scanning; multi step planning; information extraction; web search; classification; research and reports; document and explain; grade a run; others.
- tiny=true: problem, fix and check are one sentence each and a mistake shows itself at once, OR it is a pure session-control directive (status, stop, restate, commit, one-step advice). Use cats [] when tiny.
- builds=true: the work writes or edits code/config/test/doc FILES as its deliverable. Pure read/analyze/report = false.
- Use "others" only when the ask is too vague to categorize.
- Multi-label when the prompt genuinely asks for several kinds of work.

Prompts:

[p001] Where is Lb_Filter implemented? Point at the DSD file and line, then the JS file and line. One short paragraph, then a table.
